WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.960
Death is as commonplace in film as green&nbsp;
screens and fake facial hair. But in some cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.480
there's a lot of confusion surrounding&nbsp;
the death of a character. From mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:13.040
disappearances to off-screen mortalities,&nbsp;
these movie deaths were never truly explained.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.160
In Indiana Jones and the&nbsp;
Kingdom of the Crystal Skull,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.640
Sean Connery didn't return&nbsp;
as Professor Henry Jones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.040
though he'd proven a welcome addition to the Indy&nbsp;
franchise in Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:24.560
"Dad?"

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:24.960
"What?"

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:25.520
"Dad?"

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:25.920
"What?"

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:26.360
"Dad!"

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:27.360
"What?!"

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.560
"Head for the fireplace!"

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.360
We don't get a lot of information on how&nbsp;
Professor Jones died, but Indy confirms it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.440
in Kingdom of the Crystal Skull while looking&nbsp;
at a photograph of his father on his desk.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.440
We're apparently meant to assume Henry died&nbsp;
of natural causes, but the events of The Last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.560
Crusade beg for a more specific explanation. In&nbsp;
the film, the villainous Walter Donovan shoots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.360
Indy's father, leaving the Holy Grail as Henry's&nbsp;
only possible salvation. Indy finds the Grail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.280
heals Henry's wound with it, and has him&nbsp;
drink from the cup. Earlier in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:59.440
we're told the knights who discovered the&nbsp;
Grail were alive 150 years after finding it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:04.960
meaning the artifact bestows longer life. Taking&nbsp;
one little drink might not grant Henry 150 more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.200
years, but it seems reasonable to assume it'd give&nbsp;
him at least a little more time than expected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.640
Perhaps offscreen he went on another&nbsp;
adventure with Indy that he shouldn't have?

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.840
Sean Connery's Henry Jones isn't the only elder&nbsp;
academic missing from Indiana Jones and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.400
Kingdom of the Crystal Skull. Museum curator&nbsp;
and Marshall College dean of students Marcus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:27.200
Brody is missing as well, though the real-world&nbsp;
reason for his absence was incredibly tragic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.280
Denholm Elliott, the actor who played Brody&nbsp;
in Raiders of the Lost Ark and Indiana Jones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:36.400
and the Last Crusade, sadly passed away&nbsp;
in 1992 from AIDS-related tuberculosis.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:40.800
It's never mentioned exactly how Marcus dies&nbsp;
in Indy's narrative, but he isn't forgotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.200
in Kingdom of the Crystal Skull. After Marcus'&nbsp;
passing, a statue is erected in his honor on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.160
campus of Marshall College. When Indy and Mutt&nbsp;
lead KGB agents on a chase through the campus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:54.720
one of the cars crashes into Brody's memorial&nbsp;
bust. That's when the head of the statue breaks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.640
off, crashes through the car's windshield,&nbsp;
and lands in the lap of one of the KGB agents.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.920
The Autobot known as Sideswipe isn't the most&nbsp;
visible Transformer. He makes his first appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.000
in Transformers: Revenge of the Fallen, showing&nbsp;
up in Shanghai where he kills the Decepticon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.480
Sideways. Sideswipe returns in Transformers:&nbsp;
Dark of the Moon, and while he survives the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.360
second film, he's absent in both Transformers: Age&nbsp;
of Extinction and Transformers: The Last Knight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:20.880
We don't see him killed or hear anything&nbsp;
about why he isn't around anymore.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:24.720
Believe it or not, the minor hero's death is&nbsp;
revealed not on the big screen or even in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.800
behind-the-scenes feature, but on a collectible&nbsp;
trading card that reveals he was killed between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.120
movies. Of course, if it's only explained&nbsp;
in a trading card set, that means a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.960
viewers who only know him from the movies can&nbsp;
imagine Sideswipe is still out there somewhere.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.280
One of the most confusing "non-explanation&nbsp;
explanations" for a death comes in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:45.280
conclusion to the Star Wars prequels,&nbsp;
with 2005's Revenge of the Sith.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:50.000
While Anakin Skywalker is being turned into a&nbsp;
slow-breathing cyborg, his clandestine wife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.720
Padme Amidala, dies for extremely vague&nbsp;
reasons. After giving birth to twins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.320
Padme appears to be in perfect health.&nbsp;
Nevertheless, her life is still slipping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:02.640
away. So why is she dying? Well, as a&nbsp;
medical droid explains to Obi-Wan Kenobi,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:05.040
"We don't know why. She's lost the will to live."

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.040
Even if we forget for a moment that deeming&nbsp;
a person to have "lost the will to live"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:13.200
seems a fairly deep revelation for a Droid,&nbsp;
we're left with two sentences that literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.000
contradict one another. The droid first says it&nbsp;
doesn't know why she's dying, then it says why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.800
she's dying. It would be as if it said, "We don't&nbsp;
know why she's dying. Also, she was decapitated."

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.880
Still, "she lost the will to live" isn't&nbsp;
much of an explanation. We can dig deeper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.160
ourselves and theorize that it's because&nbsp;
Anakin broke her heart with his betrayal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.480
so she just didn't want to&nbsp;
live anymore. That said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:36.720
she also knew she had twin babies on the way,&nbsp;
which seems like something worth living for.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:41.360
So, the real medical explanation for exactly&nbsp;
why Padme died might remain a mystery forever.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.320
After the TV series Twin Peaks was canceled,&nbsp;
the prequel film Twin Peaks: Fire Walk with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:51.360
Me was released in 1992. The film opens on the&nbsp;
investigation into the death of Theresa Banks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.320
conducted by Agent Chet Desmond and his partner&nbsp;
Agent Stanley. Their investigation abruptly ends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.000
in the Fat Trout Trailer Park when Agent&nbsp;
Desmond discovers the victim's ring under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.840
a trailer. We see him reach for it,&nbsp;
and that's it. We never see him again.

00:04:03.840 --> 00:04:08.160
It could be that Desmond isn't dead. Instead, he&nbsp;
may have been pulled into an otherworldly place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:13.280
known as the Black Lodge. The original TV series&nbsp;
ends when Agent Dale Cooper is imprisoned there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:16.800
and in Fire Walk with Me, when Cooper&nbsp;
investigates Desmond's disappearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:20.720
he finds the words "Let's Rock" written&nbsp;
across the windshield of Desmond's car — a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:27.840
phrase spoken in the TV series by a Black Lodge&nbsp;
resident, the mysterious Man from Another Place.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:28.960
"Let's rock!"

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:33.440
In spite of the series getting a second lease&nbsp;
on life with 2017's Twin Peaks: The Return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:38.160
Desmond's fate isn't mentioned. In an AMA&nbsp;
Reddit session after the miniseries concluded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.400
co-creator Mark Frost apologetically told fans&nbsp;
that even though the character was discussed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:46.400
the showrunners couldn't come up with a way to&nbsp;
bring Desmond back, quote, "from wherever he is."

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.080
If all you know of Jack Crawford comes from the&nbsp;
award-winning 1991 film The Silence of the Lambs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:56.000
then you might not think he's a very important&nbsp;
character to the franchise. But Crawford is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.000
integral to the Thomas Harris novels that the&nbsp;
movies are based upon, and Scott Glenn plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.400
him to perfection in the first film. However,&nbsp;
Crawford fails to appear in the 2001 sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.040
Hannibal. In fact, he isn't mentioned at all in&nbsp;
the film's theatrical release. A deleted scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:13.600
confirms he died before the film's events, but&nbsp;
it doesn't mention how. In the novel Hannibal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.080
Crawford dies from a heart attack, but this&nbsp;
happens during the events of the book, not before.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:23.040
In 2015, Glenn told The A.V. Club both he and&nbsp;
Silence of the Lambs co-star Jodie Foster were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.440
asked to return for Hannibal, but they agreed&nbsp;
doing so could, quote, "ruin a good thing," so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:32.160
both declined. Still, it's unfortunate director&nbsp;
Ridley Scott was either unwilling or unable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:35.920
recast the role — or at least make some room&nbsp;
in the screenplay to explain why Crawford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:40.000
wasn't around in the theatrical release. Of&nbsp;
course, Glenn is now part of a rich tradition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.880
of acclaimed actors playing Jack Crawford. In&nbsp;
2002, Harvey Keitel took the role of Crawford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:49.120
in the prequel Red Dragon and Laurence Fishburne&nbsp;
played him in the acclaimed Hannibal TV series.

